@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2023
Match List-I with List-II and choose the correct answer using the code given below :
@match
List-I | List-II
A. Merchandise Turnover | 1. Current Assets / Current Liabilities
B. Current Ratio | 2. Liquid Assets / Current Liabilities
C. Acid Test Ratio | 3. Net Profit before Interest and Tax / Total Assets
D. Gross Capital Employed | 4. Cost of Goods Sold / Average Stock
@endmatch
Code : A B C D
(a) 3 2 1 4
(b) 4 1 2 3
(c) 4 3 1 2
(d) 2 1 3 4
@ans (*)
@note The intended matching is (b): **stock turnover = cost of goods sold / average stock**, **current ratio = current assets / current liabilities**, **acid test ratio = liquid assets / current liabilities**, and **return on total assets = EBIT / total assets**. Item D, "Gross Capital Employed", is not a ratio and does not fit item 3, so the source marks it (*).
@end

@src 8:166
@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2023
Given below are two statements, in which one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other as Reason (R):
Assertion (A) : A condition is a stipulation which is essential to the main purpose of the contract.
Reason (R) : A breach of condition gives a right to claim damages and not a right to repudiate the contract.
Select the correct answer using the code given below :
Code :
(a) (A) is false but (R) is true.
(b) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is correct explanation of (A).
(c) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).
(d) (A) is true but (R) is false.
@ans (d)
@end

Under the **Sale of Goods Act, 1930**, breaking a **condition** (an essential term) lets the buyer **reject the contract**; breaking a **warranty** (a side term) only gives a claim for **damages**.

